	"_note": "probe_ferry_catamaran with EDGE TRIM added by hand, and nothing else changed. Every entity with id < 100 is byte-identical to probe_ferry_catamaran.json; every entity with id >= 100 is trim. The question this fixture answers is whether the retired voxel skin baker's 'deck/roof strips, base skirts, corner posts' actually buy anything on top of today's butt-joined boxes, and what they cost. Four families: (a) CAP RAILS 100-102, a 0.16 band brought 0.08 proud of every bulwark top including the transom, which the original leaves bare; (b) FASCIA BANDS 103-105, a 0.28 ring hung on the edge of each deck plate that serves as a deckhouse roof, bracketing the plate so the roof edge reads as a member rather than a cut; (c) BASE SKIRTS 106-121, a coaming band where each deckhouse foot meets the deck it stands on, SPLIT AROUND EVERY DOOR so no threshold gains a 0.2 m step — this split is the reason hand-authoring does not scale, it needs the openings of the s-face wall of each room back-solved from expand_room's own extension rule; (d) CORNER POSTS 140-151, a square post centred on each deckhouse corner, buried 0.02 into the skirt below and the fascia above so no face is ever coplanar with what it lands on. (e) RUBBING STRAKE 130-133, a band around the whole 16 x 45 bridge-deck edge at the hull-deck junction. Costs nothing in draw calls: every trim entity is material 'painted' and reuses a palette colour, and the baker buckets on material alone. TWO DEFECTS FOUND IN THE ORIGINAL while measuring for this and NOT fixed here, because fixing them would confound the comparison: room 41 spans y 3.0-5.6 while its roof plate 31 spans 5.82-6.00, so the upper saloon has a 0.22 m open gap between wall top and roof; and the bulwark cap plates 20/21 are not mirrored (20 lies inboard of wall 1's centreline, 21 outboard of wall 2's).",
